Print where the toolbar ended up, not where it was put, and correct patch 0081.

PATCH 0081 STATES A CONCLUSION THAT IS WRONG AND THIS PATCH WITHDRAWS IT. It says the toolbar is
placed at NSMaxY(content) - delta and therefore sits 32 pixels INSIDE the content. The first half
is what the code assigns. The second half is false, and I could only tell because the trace printed
the value assigned rather than the value that survived.

-[NSWindow _toolbarSizeDidChangeFromOldHeight:] assigns the origin and THEN calls
setFrame:display:animate: on the window, which resizes the background view and autoresizes its
subviews. The toolbar view carries mask 0xa, NSViewWidthSizable plus NSViewMinYMargin, so that
resize moves it. Printing the frame after everything:

    setY=268.0 actual={0.0,300.0 406.0x56.0} mask=0xa content={0,0 406x300} window=406x378

The toolbar ends at y=300, which is exactly NSMaxY(content). It was ALREADY correct, autoresizing
put it there, and the subtraction of the delta never reached the screen. That is also why changing
the assignment to NSMaxY(content) moved nothing: I compared the two captures pixel for pixel and
got ZERO differing pixels out of 859104. A change that the trace confirmed had no effect on the
render at all, which is exactly what it should be if the assignment was already being overwritten.

So the placement is not the defect and #255 is not a toolbar geometry bug.

WHAT THE SAME RUN DID FIND. The Preferences window frame is 406x378 by the time the toolbar has
grown, and its Wayland surface is created at

    cider-wayland-window create=ok number=4 size=406x331 at=697,-9 level=0 style=0x3

406 by 331, FORTY SEVEN PIXELS SHORTER than the frame AppKit is laying out for, and it is never
resized afterwards. The surface is created at t=48.3, long after the toolbar sizing, so this is not
a race with the toolbar; the size the surface is born with is simply not the frame. That is the
open question in #255 now.

THE LESSON, and it is one this port has recorded before under set then unset looks exactly like
never set: an assignment is not a measurement. Print the state the consumer reads, after everything
that can touch it has run.

Task #255.

--- a/AppKit/NSWindow.m
+++ b/AppKit/NSWindow.m
@@ -1724,12 +1724,20 @@
      * toolbar labels. Task #255. */
     if (getenv("CIDER_TRACE_TOOLBAR") != NULL) {
         NSRect content = [[self contentView] frame];
+        /* WHERE IT ACTUALLY ENDED UP, not where it was put. setFrame: on the window above resizes
+         * the background view and autoresizes its subviews, so the origin assigned a few lines
+         * earlier can be overwritten before anything draws. Printing the assigned value alone made
+         * a corrected placement look effective while the render did not move by a single pixel. */
+        NSRect actual = [toolbarView frame];
 
         fprintf(stderr,
-                "CIDER_TOOLBAR old=%.1f new=%.1f delta=%.1f toolbarY=%.1f "
-                "content={%.1f,%.1f %.1fx%.1f} window=%.1fx%.1f\n",
+                "CIDER_TOOLBAR old=%.1f new=%.1f delta=%.1f setY=%.1f actual={%.1f,%.1f %.1fx%.1f} "
+                "mask=0x%lx content={%.1f,%.1f %.1fx%.1f} window=%.1fx%.1f\n",
                 (double) oldHeight, (double) newHeight, (double) contentHeightDelta,
-                (double) toolbarOrigin.y, (double) content.origin.x, (double) content.origin.y,
+                (double) toolbarOrigin.y, (double) actual.origin.x, (double) actual.origin.y,
+                (double) actual.size.width, (double) actual.size.height,
+                (unsigned long) [toolbarView autoresizingMask],
+                (double) content.origin.x, (double) content.origin.y,
                 (double) content.size.width, (double) content.size.height,
                 (double) frame.size.width, (double) frame.size.height);
         fflush(stderr);
